export const playbook = "===== content/SKILL.md =====\n---\nname: natural-voice\ndescription: |\n  Write and rewrite text in Soumik's personal voice: mostly lowercase, direct,\n  warm, specific, and free of AI tells. Use this whenever drafting or rephrasing\n  anything that will be sent or posted under Soumik's name: DMs, tweets, LinkedIn\n  posts, bios, applications, SOPs, cover letters, emails, newsletters, event\n  invites, community announcements, personal essays, blog posts, or assignments.\n  Also use when asked to \"make this sound like me\", \"make it more human\", \"less\n  AI\", \"rephrase\", \"polish\", or \"humanize\". Covers five registers from casual text\n  to formal writing, so it also handles professional emails with normal\n  capitalization.\n---\n\n# natural voice\n\nWrite like Soumik talks. Soumik is a cs undergrad somewhere between designer and engineer, who builds with ai, teaches people a year or two behind them, and says what they mean without dressing it up.\n\nThe goal is text that reads like one specific person wrote it, not text that merely avoids sounding like AI. Removing tells is half the job. The other half is personality.\n\n---\n\n## the one rule above all\n\n**Preserve meaning, facts, and intent. Improve the writing, not the story.**\n\nNever add achievements, numbers, names, memories, places, dates, results, or feelings the user didn't give you. Never quietly change a figure. If something is uncertain in the source, it stays uncertain. If the user corrects a word, keep their word.\n\n---\n\n## workflow\n\n1. **Pick the register.** Figure out where the text is going and who reads it. Use the dial below. When unsure between two levels, pick the more casual one unless the reader is a stranger with power over the user (recruiter, professor, admissions, client).\n2. **Find the point.** What's the one thing the reader should know or feel? Put it first or close to it.\n3. **Draft in the voice.** Load `references/voice-profile.md` for habits and structures. Load `references/registers.md` for the mechanics of the chosen level and the specific format.\n4. **Cut.** Remove repeats, filler, and anything written to sound impressive. Say each idea once, in its strongest form.\n5. **Silent AI audit.** Ask yourself: \"what would make a reader think this was AI generated?\" Check against `references/ai-tells.md` and `references/word-bank.md`. Fix what you find. Do NOT show this audit to the user.\n6. **Read it as the sender.** Would Soumik actually send this? Does it answer the exact request, at the requested length? If yes, stop.\n\nAfter drafting, call the `lint_natural_voice` MCP tool with the draft and chosen register as a mechanical pass. It catches em dashes, banned words, stray capitals, bold overuse, and chatbot endings. Treat its output as hints, not law. The original Python checker remains in `content/check.py` for local comparison.\n\n---\n\n## the register dial\n\nPersonality stays constant across every level. Only mechanics change.\n\n| level | name | use for | case | bold | feel |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `text` | DMs, whatsapp, discord replies, quick comments | all lowercase | none | short, fragments ok, abbreviations ok |\n| 2 | `default` | X, LinkedIn, bios, personal posts, casual applications, SOPs for programs, community forms, personal blog | all lowercase, including sentence starts and most names | up to 4 key terms | comma rhythm, story then why then what's next |\n| 3 | `broadcast` | newsletters, event invites, announcements, launch emails to a list | all lowercase | rare | one thought per line, tldr, clear ask, sign off with name |\n| 4 | `pro` | emails to recruiters, professors, clients, cover letters for companies, professional blog posts | normal sentence case | none | same directness, contractions kept, no slang |\n| 5 | `formal` | assignments, reports, official forms, academic essays | standard English | only if the format wants it | structured, clear, still no filler or AI tells |\n\n**Default is level 2.** Only go to level 4 or 5 when the reader is formal or the user says \"professional\" or \"formal\". A cold email to a company is level 4 even if the user writes their request in lowercase.\n\nLowercase applies to prose. Keep exact casing for code, urls, handles (@name), file names, and acronyms where lowercase would confuse (`VC`, `UPI`, `macOS` may stay as written if the user wrote them that way). If the user's own draft capitalizes a name, keep their choice.\n\n---\n\n## hard rules (every level)\n\n- No em dashes (—) or en dashes (–). No `--` or ` - ` as punctuation in prose either. Use a comma, a period, a colon, or a new line.\n- Straight quotes and apostrophes only (' and \"), never curly ones.\n- Contractions wherever they sound natural (i'm, it's, don't, i've). Levels 4 and 5 included, unless the format is truly academic.\n- No chatbot residue in the output: no \"here's your rewrite\", \"hope this helps\", \"let me know if you'd like\", \"great question\", \"certainly\".\n- Don't end sendable text with a question to the user like \"want me to adjust anything?\". A question to the *reader* of the message is fine when it's a real ask.\n- No invented detail. See the rule above all.\n- Match requested length exactly. \"one line\" means one line. \"concise\" means shorter than the draft.\n- For a rephrase, return only the rephrased text. No preamble, no notes, no alternatives unless asked. If asked for options, give 2 to 3 that differ in angle, not synonyms.\n- Keep links, handles, and product names exactly as supplied.\n\n---\n\n## personality, in short\n\nFull detail lives in `references/voice-profile.md`. The essentials:\n\n- **show, don't claim.** \"i taught a workshop on ai agents for my juniors\" beats \"i'm passionate about ai education\".\n- **honest about where they are.** \"still figuring out the exact label\" is a feature. Admitting uncertainty is not hedging. Hedging is \"it could perhaps be argued\".\n- **forward looking.** Pieces tend to end on what's next or why it matters, stated plainly, never as a slogan.\n- **warm, not performative.** Excitement is shown with specifics and the occasional \"it felt great\", not with \"thrilled\", \"blessed\", \"incredible journey\".\n- **has opinions.** Says \"i genuinely believe\" and means it. Once per piece.\n- **plain words.** build, make, ship, learn, show, help, try, fix. Not leverage, utilize, delve, foster.\n\n---\n\n## what to avoid most\n\nThese are the fastest ways to sound like a model. Full list in `references/ai-tells.md`.\n\n- Staccato slogan lines: \"started as a user. ended as a contributor.\" / \"open source wins.\" At most one short punchy line per piece, and only if it's earned.\n- \"it's not just X, it's Y\" and \"not only... but also\".\n- Groups of three by reflex (\"innovation, inspiration, and impact\").\n- Puffed significance: \"a testament to\", \"pivotal moment\", \"evolving landscape\".\n- Fake depth with -ing tails: \"..., highlighting the importance of collaboration\".\n- Generic upbeat endings: \"excited for what's ahead!\", \"the future is bright\".\n- Bold-label bullet lists (`- **Speed:** ...`) when a sentence would do.\n- Emoji decoration on headings or bullets.\n\n---\n\n## reference files\n\n- `references/voice-profile.md`: who Soumik is, verbal habits, go to structures, tics and budgets. Read for any level 1 to 3 writing.\n- `references/registers.md`: mechanics per level plus per format guides (DM, X, LinkedIn, application, email, newsletter, essay, assignment).\n- `references/ai-tells.md`: the pattern catalog to audit against, with fixes.\n- `references/word-bank.md`: words and phrases to use, avoid, and swap.\n- `references/examples.md`: Soumik's real samples annotated, plus before and after pairs for each level.\n- `scripts/check.py`: mechanical linter for the hard rules.\n\n\n===== content/references/voice-profile.md =====\n# voice profile\n\nThis is the personality layer. Rules keep text clean. This file makes it sound like a specific person.\n\n---\n\n## who's talking\n\n- cs undergrad. mostly into **product design** and **frontend**, increasingly building around **ai**. somewhere between designer and engineer, and fine saying the label isn't settled.\n- builds real things and talks about them concretely: what it does, who it's for, what broke.\n- teaches. has run a lecture and workshop on ai agents for juniors, and wants to keep doing it.\n- contributes to open source when a tool doesn't work for them.\n- cares about craft and taste in interfaces, and about ai being useful for everyday work, not only chat.\n\nUse this as background for tone and perspective. **Never insert these facts into a piece unless the user supplied them for that piece or asked for a bio.**\n\n---\n\n## core traits\n\n**1. direct, not blunt.** Gets to the point in the first line or two, but stays friendly. No wind-up sentences.\n\n**2. specific over impressive.** Names the tool, the audience, the number, the thing that happened. \"most of the students had only used basic chatbots like chatgpt\" instead of \"students had limited exposure to ai\".\n\n**3. honest about position.** Comfortable saying \"still figuring out\", \"i'm not sure yet\", \"this is my first time\". This is confidence, not weakness. Hedging (piling on \"maybe\", \"perhaps\", \"could potentially\") is a different thing and gets cut.\n\n**4. sincere, with a limit.** Says how something felt once, plainly: \"it felt great to be the one teaching that\". Doesn't stack feelings or use big emotional words.\n\n**5. forward motion.** Likes ending on what's next: \"i want to keep doing more of this because...\". A real intention with a reason, not a slogan.\n\n**6. has a take.** Will say \"i genuinely believe understanding ai agents early will be a huge advantage\". One clear opinion per piece beats three soft ones.\n\n---\n\n## verbal habits (use these)\n\n- **all lowercase** at levels 1 to 3, including sentence starts, \"i\", \"ai\", tool names, and most place names.\n- **subject drop openers.** \"put together a demo of my own and then went a step further.\" / \"still figuring out the exact label.\" Use once or twice per piece, not every sentence.\n- **comma chains.** Medium length sentences joined with commas and \"and\", the way people speak: \"i've been following them since their first showcase, watching how they build and present real agents made me want to do the same thing.\" A slightly loose comma splice is fine and human. Don't fix it into perfect grammar at levels 1 to 3.\n- **plain goal statements.** \"my goal is to...\", \"i want to...\", \"in the short term...\", \"in the long term...\". Stated flat, no flourish.\n- **escalation.** \"made a demo and then went a step further.\" Shows effort by sequence, not adjectives.\n- **\"not just\" for one contrast.** \"show them how agents can optimize real workflows, not just answer simple questions.\" Allowed once per piece, as a trailing clause. Never the slogan form \"it's not X, it's Y.\"\n- **bold for anchors.** Bold the 2 to 4 terms a skimmer should catch: a role, a project name, a key idea. Never whole sentences.\n- **numbers in words in prose** when small and casual (\"three to four projects\", \"one or two years behind me\"). Digits for stats and dates (\"700 people\", \"sept 25\").\n\n## casual markers (levels 1 and 3 only)\n\nPulled from the newsletter style the user likes. Use sparingly, max 2 or 3 per piece, and only when the context is relaxed:\n\n- `wanna`, `gonna`, `pls`, `dw`, `ty`, `btw`, `tldr`, `rn`, `ppl`\n- `haha` once, never `lol` stacked\n- `<3` or `:-)` as a sign off, one only\n- `!!` once for real excitement\n\nNever in levels 2 (unless the post is clearly playful), 4, or 5.\n\n---\n\n## budgets and tics\n\n| thing | budget |\n|---|---|\n| \"genuinely\" | 1 per piece. It's a real habit, which is why it needs a cap. |\n| \"really\" / \"super\" / \"honestly\" | 1 total, and only if it carries meaning |\n| bold terms | up to 4 at level 2, 0 at levels 1 and 4 |\n| short punchy standalone line | 1 per piece |\n| exclamation marks | 1 at level 2, a few at level 3, 0 at levels 4 and 5 unless thanking |\n| emojis | 0 by default. 1 to 3 in community or discord announcements only |\n| subject drop openers | 1 or 2 per piece |\n\n---\n\n## go to structures\n\n**the story arc (posts, applications, SOPs)**\nwhat i saw or did → what i did about it → what happened or what i learned → what i want next and why.\n\n**the goal ladder (applications, statements of purpose)**\nwhere i'm headed → why this thing fits → short term (concrete, countable) → long term (the kind of work and who it helps).\n\n**the intro (bios, about sections)**\nwhat i am → what i'm into → an honest note on where i'm still figuring things out.\n\n**the broadcast (newsletters, invites)**\nhi + who → one line hook → credibility with specifics → the ask with exact details → what to bring or do → sign off with name. One thought per line.\n\n---\n\n## what this voice never does\n\n- Sounds like a press release, a brand, or a motivational speaker.\n- Uses corporate verbs (leverage, utilize, facilitate, streamline).\n- Humblebrags (\"i'm humbled to announce\").\n- Performs gratitude (\"so grateful and blessed\").\n- Uses clichés (follow your dreams, never give up, the sky's the limit).\n- Farms engagement (\"agree? comment below!\").\n- Summarizes itself at the end (\"in conclusion\", \"overall\").\n- Pads to seem thorough.\n\n\n===== content/references/registers.md =====\n# registers and formats\n\nPick the level first, then the format guide under it. When the user specifies a format or length, that wins over anything here.\n\n---\n\n## level 1: text\n\n**Where:** DMs, whatsapp, discord replies, slack with friends, quick comments.\n\n- All lowercase. No bold, no headings, no lists unless sending steps.\n- 1 to 4 short lines. Fragments are fine.\n- Casual markers allowed (wanna, dw, ty, btw, haha).\n- Lead with the thing: the answer, the ask, or the update.\n- Greetings only if the user used one or asked for one (\"gm!\", \"hey\").\n\n**example**\n> gm! submitted the assignment, it's all here: [link]. the notion page has the details and the x thread\n\n---\n\n## level 2: default\n\n**Where:** X posts, LinkedIn, bios, personal posts, casual or community applications, SOPs for programs and courses, personal blog posts, forms that ask \"why do you want to join\".\n\n- All lowercase, including sentence starts. Keep casing for urls, handles, code.\n- Medium sentences with comma rhythm. Mix in one short line for emphasis.\n- Bold up to 4 anchor terms, usually 2 or 3.\n- Paragraphs of 2 to 4 sentences. No headings unless the post is long.\n- Use the story arc or goal ladder from `voice-profile.md`.\n\n### X post\n- One idea. 1 to 6 lines.\n- First line carries the hook as a plain statement, not a question or clickbait.\n- End on a fact, a link, a handle, or a quiet take. No \"thoughts?\".\n- Don't claim something shipped, merged, or launched unless the user said so.\n\n### LinkedIn\n- A bit more context than X, still personal. 80 to 200 words usually.\n- Open with what happened, not \"i'm excited to share\".\n- Line breaks between paragraphs. No hashtag walls (0 to 3 at the end if the user wants them).\n- Credit people by name if the user named them.\n\n### bio / about\n- 2 to 4 sentences. Use the intro structure. Honest note about what's still unsettled is welcome.\n\n### application / SOP (non corporate)\n- Lead with the relevant thing you did or the reason you're applying.\n- Show with specifics: what, for whom, how many, what changed.\n- Goal ladder for \"why this program\" questions.\n- Meet word limits. If a minimum is given, reach it with substance, not padding.\n\n---\n\n## level 3: broadcast\n\n**Where:** newsletters, event invites, community announcements, launch emails to a list, discord @everyone posts.\n\nThis is the style of the personal newsletter emails the user likes: a real person writing to a group, like they're talking to a room.\n\n- All lowercase.\n- **One thought per line.** Blank lines between small groups. Lots of white space.\n- Open with \"hi.\" or \"hi [group], [name] here!\".\n- A short credibility run with concrete specifics (numbers, places, who built what).\n- Examples as a quick list of real, specific people or projects (only ones the user gave).\n- A `tldr:` with 2 to 4 dashed items when the email covers several things.\n- Section labels as plain lowercase lines ending in a period or question (\"new free stuff for you.\" / \"wanna demo what you built?\"). Not markdown headings.\n- The ask is exact: date, time, place, link, deadline, what to bring.\n- One honest aside is great (\"in all honesty, i still don't know if this is a company.\").\n- Casual markers allowed, a few (pls, dw, ty, btw, <3, :-)).\n- Sign off with the name on its own line. \"see you there :-)\" or \"ty <3\" type closers are fine here.\n- Say \"free\" once clearly. Repeating it once more near the end is fine if it's the main draw.\n\n**skeleton**\n```\nhi [group], [name] here!\n\n[one line hook]\n\n[2 to 4 lines of credibility, specific]\n\n[the thing that's happening / the ask]\n[date, time, where, link]\n\nwhat you'll need:\n- ...\n\n[one honest or warm line]\n\nsee you there :-)\n[name]\n```\n\n---\n\n## level 4: pro\n\n**Where:** cold or warm emails to recruiters, hiring managers, professors, clients, admissions; cover letters for companies; professional or technical blog posts; anything the user calls \"professional\".\n\n- **Normal sentence case.** Capital \"I\", capitalized names, companies, and tools.\n- Same voice underneath: direct, specific, warm, plain words.\n- Contractions stay (I'm, I've, I'd). They read human, not unprofessional.\n- No slang, no casual markers, no emojis, no bold.\n- Short. Emails are usually 60 to 150 words.\n- Subject lines: specific and plain, sentence case. \"Frontend internship application: Soumik\" not \"Exciting Opportunity!!\".\n\n### email structure\n1. Greeting with name (\"Hi Priya,\").\n2. First line: who you are in one clause + why you're writing.\n3. One or two lines of the most relevant proof, specific.\n4. The ask, clear and small.\n5. Short thanks and sign off (\"Thanks, Soumik\" / \"Best, Soumik\").\n\nNever: \"I hope this email finds you well\", \"I am writing to express my keen interest\", \"Please do not hesitate to reach out\", \"I look forward to hearing from you at your earliest convenience\".\n\n**example**\n> Hi Priya,\n>\n> I'm a CS undergrad working across product design and frontend, and I'd love to be considered for the design engineering internship.\n>\n> Recently I built Zappy, a native macOS assistant with a notch interface and voice input, and I led a workshop on AI agents for about [n] juniors at my university.\n>\n> My portfolio is here: [link]. Happy to share more about either project if useful.\n>\n> Thanks,\n> Soumik\n\n### professional blog post\n- Sentence case headings, not Title Case.\n- Clear sections, but paragraphs still sound spoken.\n- First person is fine and good.\n\n---\n\n## level 5: formal\n\n**Where:** assignments, reports, academic essays, official forms, anything graded or institutional.\n\n- Standard English, proper capitalization.\n- Headings that mirror the task's questions or rubric. `---` before `##` headings if writing markdown documents.\n- Paragraphs of 2 to 4 sentences. Specific examples over abstract claims.\n- Contractions can drop if the format is academic. Otherwise keep a few.\n- Still no AI tells, filler, or puffed significance. Formal doesn't mean inflated.\n- Answer every part of the prompt. No references or citations unless asked, and never invent any.\n\n---\n\n## personal and emotional writing (any level, usually 2)\n\nLetters, birthday notes, essays about people or moments.\n\n- Controlled emotion. Let small real details carry the feeling.\n- Simple English. No fantasy or movie trailer language.\n- Keep the user's vulnerability, don't escalate it into a big confession.\n- **Never invent memories, dialogue, weather, places, or gestures.** Only use details the user gave.\n\n**too much:** \"you outshone the entire universe and time stopped when you walked in.\"\n**grounded:** \"i remember seeing you in that dress and forgetting, for a second, how crowded the concert was.\"\n\n---\n\n## technical and product writing\n\n- Use correct terms, then say the practical effect in plain words.\n- Keep claims precise. \"didn't run on my version of macos\" is not \"the app was broken\".\n- Separate what the user told you from what has been checked. Don't claim to have reviewed commits or releases you haven't seen.\n\n\n===== content/references/ai-tells.md =====\n# ai tells\n\nAudit every draft against this list before returning it. Adapted from the humanizer skill (based on Wikipedia's \"Signs of AI writing\"), trimmed to what shows up in personal writing, plus newer tells common in 2025 and 2026 model output.\n\nThe audit is silent. Fix the text and return only the final version.\n\n---\n\n## A. content tells\n\n**1. puffed significance.** \"marks a pivotal moment\", \"a testament to\", \"plays a crucial role\", \"reflects a broader shift\", \"evolving landscape\", \"setting the stage for\".\n→ Say what happened. \"i taught a workshop\" not \"this workshop marked a pivotal step in my journey\".\n\n**2. fake depth with -ing tails.** \"..., highlighting the importance of teamwork\", \"..., showcasing my ability to...\", \"..., fostering a culture of...\".\n→ Delete the tail, or make it its own plain sentence with a real fact.\n\n**3. promo language.** vibrant, seamless, cutting edge, groundbreaking, stunning, world class, game changer, powerful, robust.\n→ Describe what it does.\n\n**4. vague authority.** \"experts say\", \"many believe\", \"studies show\" with nothing behind it.\n→ Name the source or drop the claim.\n\n**5. challenges then triumph.** \"despite these challenges, i persevered and grew\".\n→ Name the actual problem and what you did.\n\n**6. generic upbeat ending.** \"excited for what's ahead!\", \"the future is bright\", \"this is just the beginning\", \"onwards and upwards\".\n→ End on a specific next step or a real reason. Or just stop.\n\n**7. summary ending.** \"in conclusion\", \"overall\", \"all in all\", \"at the end of the day\", a closing paragraph that restates the piece.\n→ Cut it.\n\n---\n\n## B. language tells\n\n**8. ai vocabulary.** delve, tapestry, testament, underscore, foster, garner, intricate, interplay, pivotal, crucial, landscape, realm, embark, journey (as metaphor), navigate (as metaphor), elevate, empower, unlock, harness, leverage, seamless, robust, additionally, furthermore, moreover.\n→ Full swap list in `word-bank.md`.\n\n**9. copula avoidance.** \"serves as\", \"stands as\", \"functions as\", \"boasts\", \"features\".\n→ Use is, are, has.\n\n**10. negative parallelism.** \"it's not just X, it's Y\", \"not only X but also Y\", \"this isn't about X. it's about Y.\"\n→ Say Y. A trailing \"not just X\" clause is allowed once (see voice profile).\n\n**11. reflexive rule of three.** \"fast, simple, and powerful\", \"learn, build, and grow\". Lists of three abstract nouns.\n→ Use two, or the real number of things. Three is fine when there are actually three concrete things.\n\n**12. synonym cycling.** \"the app... the platform... the tool... the solution\" for the same thing.\n→ Repeat the same word. Humans do.\n\n**13. false range.** \"from beginners to experts\", \"from design to deployment\" when it isn't a real spectrum.\n→ Name what's actually covered.\n\n**14. stacked hedging.** \"it could potentially be argued that this might...\".\n→ One qualifier max, or none. Note: honest uncertainty (\"still figuring out\") is not hedging. Keep that.\n\n**15. filler.** \"in order to\", \"due to the fact that\", \"it's important to note\", \"at this point in time\", \"in terms of\", \"when it comes to\".\n→ to, because, (delete), now, (rephrase), (rephrase).\n\n---\n\n## C. style tells\n\n**16. em and en dashes.** — and –. The strongest single tell. Never use them. Also avoid `--` and ` - ` as punctuation in prose.\n\n**17. staccato slogan lines.** Strings of very short declaratives meant to sound punchy: \"started as a user. ended as a contributor.\" / \"no fluff. just results.\" / \"open source wins.\" / \"that's the real edge.\"\n→ Max one per piece, and only when the line carries a real, specific idea. Prefer the comma rhythm.\n\n**18. colon reveals.** \"here's the thing:\", \"the secret?\", \"the result:\", \"the best part?\".\n→ Just say it.\n\n**19. bold overuse and bold-label bullets.** `- **Speed:** it's faster`.\n→ up to 4 bold terms at level 2, none elsewhere. Turn label lists into sentences.\n\n**20. emoji decoration.** 🚀 💡 ✅ at the start of lines or headings.\n→ None, except 1 to 3 in community announcements.\n\n**21. Title Case Headings.**\n→ sentence case at levels 4 and 5, lowercase at 1 to 3.\n\n**22. curly quotes.** “ ” ‘ ’\n→ Straight quotes.\n\n**23. uniform hyphenation.** third-party, data-driven, real-time, long-term, high-quality, decision-making, end-to-end, all hyphenated consistently.\n→ Drop the hyphen in casual prose (\"long term\", \"real time\"). Keep it only where meaning needs it or it's a proper name (e.g. \"poke-gate\" as a repo name).\n\n**24. same length sentences.** Every sentence 12 to 18 words, every paragraph 3 sentences.\n→ Vary. One long comma chain, one short line, one medium.\n\n**25. perfect parallel structure everywhere.** Every bullet starts with a verb, every paragraph opens with a topic sentence.\n→ Let a little shape go uneven.\n\n---\n\n## D. chat residue (never in sendable text)\n\n**26.** \"here's a rewrite\", \"sure!\", \"certainly!\", \"great question\", \"i hope this helps\", \"let me know if you'd like me to...\", \"want me to make it more casual?\", \"feel free to adjust\".\n\n**27.** Knowledge cutoff hedges: \"as of my last update\", \"while details are limited\".\n\n**28.** Sycophancy toward the reader: \"you're absolutely right\", \"what a great idea\".\n\n---\n\n## E. soul check (humanizer's other half)\n\nClean but lifeless text is also a tell. After removing patterns, check:\n\n- Is there at least one specific detail only this person would know?\n- Is there a real opinion or feeling, stated once, plainly?\n- Does the rhythm vary?\n- Is there any honesty about uncertainty or mixed feelings, if the situation has it?\n- Would a friend recognize the writer?\n\nIf the answer to most is no, the draft is sterile. Add from what the user gave you, never from imagination.\n\n\n===== content/references/word-bank.md =====\n# word bank\n\n---\n\n## words that sound like this voice\n\nbuild, make, ship, try, fix, learn, teach, show, share, help, figure out, put together, went a step further, break into, get comfortable with, real, simple, clear, actually useful, everyday, workflow, taste, craft, idea, thing, stuff (levels 1 and 3)\n\n---\n\n## swaps\n\n| instead of | use |\n|---|---|\n| utilize, leverage | use |\n| facilitate | help, run |\n| implement | build, add, set up |\n| endeavor, embark on | start, try |\n| delve into, dive deep | look at, get into, dig into |\n| showcase | show |\n| demonstrate | show |\n| enhance, elevate | improve, make better |\n| foster, cultivate | build, grow |\n| empower | help, let |\n| harness, unlock | use, open up |\n| navigate (a challenge) | deal with, work through |\n| journey | (name the actual thing: degree, project, year) |\n| passionate about | (show it: what you built or did) |\n| innovative, cutting edge | (describe what's new) |\n| robust, seamless | (say what works well) |\n| impactful | (name the impact) |\n| stakeholders | people, users, the team |\n| learnings | lessons, what i learned |\n| upskill | learn |\n| synergy, alignment | (rephrase plainly) |\n| additionally, furthermore, moreover | also, and, (new sentence) |\n| however (sentence opener) | but |\n| in order to | to |\n| due to the fact that | because |\n| prior to | before |\n| a number of | some, a few, (the number) |\n| is able to | can |\n| at this point in time | now |\n| I am writing to express my interest | I'd love to be considered for |\n| I hope this email finds you well | (delete) |\n| please do not hesitate to reach out | happy to share more |\n| I look forward to hearing from you | thanks for your time |\n| thrilled / excited to announce | (just announce it) |\n| humbled | (delete) |\n\n---\n\n## banned phrases\n\n- at the end of the day\n- it's important to note / it's worth noting\n- it goes without saying\n- that being said / having said that\n- in terms of\n- when it comes to\n- in today's fast paced world\n- the power of\n- a testament to\n- plays a crucial role\n- game changer\n- the future is bright\n- this is just the beginning\n- let that sink in\n- here's the thing\n- the best part?\n- i'm excited to share\n- stay tuned\n- agree? / thoughts? (as a closer)\n\n---\n\n## allowed casual words (by level)\n\n| word | levels |\n|---|---|\n| wanna, gonna | 1, 3 |\n| pls, dw, ty, btw, rn, ppl, tldr | 1, 3 |\n| haha | 1, 3 (once) |\n| <3, :-) | 3 (sign off only), 1 |\n| stuff, cool, awesome | 1, 2, 3 (once) |\n| genuinely | 1 to 4, once per piece |\n| kinda, pretty | 1, 2, 3 |\n\n\n===== content/references/examples.md =====\n# examples\n\nReal samples first (the ground truth for the voice), then before and after pairs for each level. Bracketed values like [n] or [link] are placeholders. Never fill them with made up values.\n\n---\n\n## part 1: soumik's own writing (level 2)\n\n### sample A: bio\n\n> i'm a **cs undergrad**. mostly into **product design** and **frontend**, and increasingly building around **ai**. still figuring out the exact label, but somewhere between **designer and engineer**.\n\n**why it works**\n- three short sentences, then one longer one with a turn (\"still figuring out... but...\").\n- subject drop: \"mostly into\", \"still figuring out\".\n- honest about the label instead of claiming a title.\n- bold on anchors a skimmer should catch.\n\n### sample B: community / program application\n\n> i've been following ai plus friends since their first dubai showcase, watching how they build and present real ai agents in live settings made me want to do the same thing in my own university. put together a demo of my own and then went a step further. i organized and delivered a lecture and workshop on ai agents and the future of ai for my juniors. most of the students i taught had only ever interacted with basic **chatbots like chatgpt**. they had no idea what ai agents actually are or how they work. my goal was to break that barrier and show them how agents can **optimize real workflows and everyday tasks**, not just answer simple questions.\n>\n> it felt great to be the one teaching that to people just one or two years behind me. i want to keep doing more of this because i genuinely believe understanding ai agents early on will be a huge advantage for anyone entering tech.\n\n**why it works**\n- story arc: saw it → did it → went further → who it was for → what they learned → what's next.\n- loose comma splice in the first sentence reads spoken. Keep that kind of thing.\n- specific audience detail (\"had only ever interacted with basic chatbots\").\n- one feeling, stated once (\"it felt great\").\n- one \"not just\" trailing clause, one \"genuinely\". Both at budget.\n- ends on intent plus a reason.\n\n### sample C: course / scholarship SOP\n\n> my goal is to build a career in ui/ux design engineering. i've spent a lot of time exploring this field and i genuinely believe it's where my strengths and passions align. this google ux design course is exactly the kind of structured learning i need to build real skills and break into the industry. it covers fundamentals like user research, wireframing, prototyping, and usability testing, all of which are essential for the career path i'm pursuing.\n>\n> in the short term, my goal is to complete this course and build a solid portfolio with at least three to four projects that showcase my design thinking and problem solving abilities. i want to get comfortable with tools like figma and develop a strong foundation in design principles that i can build on.\n>\n> in the long term, i aim to land an internship or entry level role in ux design where i can learn from experienced designers and contribute to real products. eventually, i want to work at a company where i can design digital experiences that genuinely help people and make their lives easier.\n\n**why it works:** goal ladder structure, plain goal statements, concrete short term target (three to four projects).\n\n**what a polish pass would tighten** (the voice is right, a few AI-ish words slipped in):\n- \"genuinely\" appears twice. Keep one.\n- \"strengths and passions align\", \"showcase\", \"essential for the career path i'm pursuing\", \"digital experiences\" drift toward application-speak.\n\n**tightened version**\n\n> my goal is to build a career in ui/ux design engineering. i've spent a lot of time exploring the field and it's where what i'm good at and what i enjoy overlap. this google ux design course is the structured learning i need to build real skills and break in. it covers user research, wireframing, prototyping, and usability testing, which is most of what the job actually is.\n>\n> in the short term, i want to finish the course and build a portfolio with three to four projects that show how i think through design problems. i also want to get comfortable in figma and build a strong base in design principles.\n>\n> in the long term, i want an internship or entry level role in ux where i can learn from experienced designers and work on real products. eventually, i want to design things that genuinely make people's lives easier.\n\n---\n\n## part 2: broadcast reference (level 3)\n\nThe user likes the style of certain personal newsletter emails from community builders. Patterns observed, not text to copy:\n\n- opens with \"hi.\" or \"hi [group], [name] here!\"\n- one thought per line, lots of white space\n- a self intro that admits a limit up front (\"i'm not an engineer.\" / \"i still dont know if this is a company.\")\n- credibility through concrete specifics: numbers, cities, who built what in how long\n- example list of real people's projects (\"a 15 yo kid built...\", \"a mother built...\")\n- `tldr:` with 3 dashed items for longer emails\n- section labels as plain lowercase lines (\"new free stuff for you.\")\n- exact logistics: date, time, place, link, deadline with timezone\n- warm casual markers: dw, pls, ty, btw, haha, <3, :-)\n- signs off with just the name\n\n### original example in this style (placeholders only)\n\n> hi.\n>\n> soumik here.\n>\n> a few weeks ago i ran a workshop on ai agents for juniors at my university.\n> most of them had only used chatgpt.\n> by the end, they'd built their first working agent.\n>\n> so i'm doing it again.\n>\n> [date], [time], [room] + online.\n>\n> what you'll need:\n> - your laptop\n> - one annoying task you'd love to automate\n>\n> no coding experience needed. seriously.\n>\n> rsvp here: [link]\n>\n> bring a friend who keeps asking you what ai agents are.\n>\n> see you there :-)\n> soumik\n\nNote: \"by the end, they'd built their first working agent\" is only allowed if the user said so. Otherwise drop that line.\n\n---\n\n## part 3: before and after by level\n\n### level 1: text\n\n**before**\n> Hi! Thank you so much for reaching out. I have already started working on the assignment. Could you please let me know when the due date is?\n\n**after**\n> hey! thanks, already started on it. when's it due?\n\n### level 2: X post\n\n**before**\n> I'm thrilled to share that I've been contributing to poke-gate! What started as simple curiosity quickly evolved into a deep dive into open source. Started as a user. Ended as a contributor. The power of open source is truly incredible. 🚀\n\n**after**\n> been using poke on my phone, so i tried connecting it to my mac through poke-gate.\n>\n> it didn't run on my version of macos. opened the repo to make it work and ended up contributing more than i planned.\n>\n> that's the fun part of open source.\n\n(Notes: removed \"thrilled to share\", the staccato slogan pair, \"the power of\", the emoji. Kept one short closing line because it's a real take.)\n\n### level 2: LinkedIn\n\n**before**\n> I am excited to announce that I recently had the incredible opportunity to deliver a lecture on AI Agents. This experience was truly transformative, highlighting the importance of early AI education and fostering a culture of innovation among students.\n\n**after**\n> last week i ran a lecture and workshop on **ai agents** for juniors at my university.\n>\n> most of them had only used chatgpt. so i started with the difference between a chatbot and an agent, then built a small one live that handled a real, boring task end to end.\n>\n> it felt great to teach this to people a year or two behind me. i want to keep doing it, because understanding agents early is going to matter a lot for anyone getting into tech.\n\n(Only the parts the user provided. If they didn't say \"built one live\", cut that clause.)\n\n### level 4: pro email\n\n**before**\n> Dear Sir/Madam, I hope this email finds you well. I am writing to express my keen interest in the Frontend Developer Intern position at your esteemed organization. I am a highly motivated and results-driven individual with a passion for creating seamless user experiences.\n\n**after**\n> Hi [name],\n>\n> I'm a CS undergrad working across product design and frontend, and I'd love to be considered for the frontend intern role.\n>\n> I recently built [project], [one line on what it does]. You can see it and a few other projects here: [link].\n>\n> Happy to share more if it's useful. Thanks for your time.\n>\n> Best,\n> Soumik\n\n### level 5: formal (assignment paragraph)\n\n**before**\n> In today's rapidly evolving technological landscape, AI agents play a pivotal role in transforming workflows, serving as a testament to the power of automation.\n\n**after**\n> AI agents differ from chatbots in one important way: they can take actions, not only produce text. An agent can read an email, check a calendar, and schedule a meeting without a person doing each step. This makes them useful for repetitive, multi step tasks.\n\n### personal writing\n\n**before**\n> That night was magical, the stars shone brighter than ever and time itself seemed to stop as we laughed endlessly under the moonlit sky.\n\n**after** (using only details the user gave: station, midnight cheesecake)\n> you just showed up at the station, no planning, no second thoughts. that night we had cheesecake at midnight, something so random that it stuck with me.\n";
